@jansel pointed out that AOTAutograd's intermediate_base logic is not great for inductor performance: it can prevent inductor from fusing things, and potentially force inductor to generate extra copies. In particular, it's preventing the ability to swap out an attention pattern for flash attention
As a quick recap, "intermediate base" handling was original created to handle graph that exhibit the below structure. If you have an autograd.Function that creates an intermediate and returns a view of the intermediate, autograd will yell at you if you attempt to mutate the graph intermediate.
class MyFunc(torch.autograd.Function):
    @staticmethod
    def forward(ctx, x):
        intermediate = x + 1
        return intermediate.view(-1)

    @staticmethod
    def backward(ctx, *args):
        raise RuntimeError("UNUSED")


a = torch.ones(2, requires_grad=True)
out = MyFunc.apply(a)
out.mul_(2)

To deal with this, AOTAutograd works pretty hard to detect "intermediate bases", and force the compiler to return intermediate bases as outputs. It then uses those intermediate to manually regenerate the output views, in an epilogue that runs after the compiled graph, outside of the custom autograd.Function.
As you can see in the above case though, we don't really care about the view relationship. In fact, in the above case it would be fine if we completely hid the fact that the output was a view.
The case where this does matter though is if you have two outputs that alias each other. This case is probably much rarer, but will require us to carefully preserve aliasing relationships:
def f(x):
    intermediate = x + 1
    return intermediate, intermediate.view(-1)

Proposal
There are several ways to deal with this. To unblock, I'll propose what I think is the simplest proposal: keep doing intermediate_base logic in the rarer case of having outputs that alias each other, but attempt to hide the aliasing relationships from autograd otherwise.
(1) AOTAutograd should detect for cases when two outputs of the program are aliased to each other.
(2) If there are any, then we'll continue doing the intermediate-base logic we have today
(3) If there are not any, then:
(a) we will not generate any intermediate_base logic.
(b) For any output that is an alias of an intermediate, we will add an out = aten._unsafe_view(out.shape) to the graph, hiding the alias relationship from autograd.
